there's a very interesting report and  truth out about the reality that just a  ton of Americans are heading to Mexico  for medical treatment because of the  high quality and relatively low-cost  care that they can just pay  out-of-pocket for their instead of  getting care in the United States so  this is a different sort of Caravan than  the one that we've been hearing about on  Fox News and from Donald Trump this is a  caravan you're not going to hear that  much about but I've actually been  following this for a very long time  medical tourism is a thing it's a sort  of arbitrage arbitrage when in general  what arbitrage means is you take  advantage of the different costs of the  same thing in different places so this  could be buying a product in one place  and then bringing it elsewhere and  selling it that might be one type of  arbitrage that you can do or if you can  borrow money from someone at 2% and then  invest it at two and a half percent  guaranteed risk-free you can leverage  that and gain 0.5 percent interest that  would be another form of arbitrage and  here we're talking about healthcare  services which means that even when you  account for travel costs Americans can  still save a lot of money by getting  health services in other countries  compared to in the United States this  includes in Asia in some cases this  includes in Europe in some cases in some  cases it's Mexico and that's the one  we're talking about today and of course  it depends on which country what type of  healthcare services you're looking for  when it comes to Mexico  it is a lot of dentistry and quite a bit  of optometry as well there are 6,000  Americans each day that are crossing the  Yuma Arizona  border crossing to go to the Mexican  town of Los Algodones which translates  literally as Cotton's to get care mostly  dental work and no cellular onus has  more dentists per capita than anywhere  in the world I actually took a  screenshot of Google Maps above the town  this is just that one zoom level if I  had zoomed in I might have been able to  find even more dental offices but I've  served  for people who are watching right now in  like a three block by two block radius  roughly there are at least six dentistry  clinics and a dental school just in this  small area this is a tiny town of 5,000  people you might also notice on this map  that there are a lot of optometrists and  pharmacies as well and a lot of what's  going on here is just demand  I mean dental care is a huge problem in  the United States there are 74 million  people in the United States who don't  have dental insurance many are not by  choice so like I've mentioned before on  the show I stopped I chose to stop  having dental insurance when I was 26  and was removed or lost coverage under  my parents plan and I made that choice  because I figured out okay based on the  fact that most most dental insurance  costs between three and five hundred  dollars per year and often has a yearly  maximum coverage of a thousand or  fifteen hundred dollars aside from your  two regular cleanings a year based on me  getting two cleanings and having like  one cavity filled every year and a half  or two years or so I figured I'll just  save the money on the insurance and pay  out-of-pocket but that's a choice on  making right it's not out of necessity  and a lot of people don't have dental  insurance for other reasons and when you  look at the cost over the counter of a  lot of procedures in the United States  you might have a two hundred two  thousand dollar rather crown you might  have a five thousand dollar dental  implant elderly folks sometimes will  have to have a number of crowns done at  once or a number of implants done at  once and it is just way cheaper and  still very good care to go to Mexico to  do this if you look at some of the  storefronts in the Mexican town across  from Yuma Arizona of Los Algodones you  see that they're totally set up for  Americans right here is a storefront  from Google Maps Street View at the  molar dental clinic which says that they  provide the face of a great smile they  offer implants crowns root canals ten  and partials it's all in English right  so this is set up for Americans known to  have terrible dental care so why does  this matter right like why in the  context of the so called border crisis  does this matter  Americans legally going to Mexico for  health care doesn't change that it's  illegal to come to the United States  illegally but consider the scale for a  second okay we are told by US Customs  and Border Protection that 20,000 to  60,000 people per month are crossing  from Mexico to the United States in this  one town in Mexico almost five times as  many health care tourists are going into  Mexico to get work done there are more  people leaving the american health care  system to get care in mexico just in  this one town than the number that's  coming to the u.s. from Mexico and  Central America fleeing violence and  we're supposed to pretend that the  crisis is people coming here rather than  the abject failures of our health care  system and one of the richest countries  in the United States and of course the  irony is or maybe better said the double  standard is that the Trump  administration is lobbying for a wall to  protect Americans from mostly refugees  and people looking for work while we  want to be able to go there to  circumvent the terrible health care  system of the United States whenever we  want the real crisis here Pat is the  health care system and that is what I  think is completely missing from the  conversation yes strictly speaking what  difference does it make in terms of what  our immigration policy should be that  the health care system is so messed up  that people are crossing to Mexico to  get dental work fine point taken but the  scope of what's going on is completely  disproportionate to the crisis that we  were told exists yeah building a  physical wall will not only keep people  from coming into the country yeah I  guess in theory you would keep people  from going out of the country too it  goes both ways they would be kind of  like the Berlin Wall in that sense and  yeah we shouldn't be surprised by this  immigration to Mexico is below Net Zero  meaning more people are going out than  coming in this so-called immigration  crisis isn't a crisis at all and this is  a very interesting story that you're  bringing up here it's important to  mention that this healthcare tourism's  not for all conditions so for example if  you consider like plastic surgery which  is mostly elective procedures but not  all there are lots of examples of people  finding significantly cheaper plastic  surgery in other countries but that can  be a much more complex thing if you want  to be able to get full care when it  comes to plastic surgery it's often  beneficial to stay for aftercare in the  case of complications where the doctor  that did the procedure can see that  through to the very end there are many  situations of people ending up with  complications and then going to an  American doctor for a cosmetic procedure  they had elsewhere and the American  doctor of course doesn't know the  details of the case and how the  operation was initially done so I'm not  someone saying travel to wherever it's  cheapest and just get care what I am  saying is that there are places that are  very good and much more affordable for  particular procedures Mexico and in  particular this region of Mexico is very  good and affordable when it comes to  dental work and a lot of those  procedures are ones that inherently come  with fewer complications not medical  advice but just a report of what it is  that is taking place let me know what  you think I'm on Twitter at D Pakman and  the show is on twitter at david pakman  show we will go to the phones we've got  a great interview for you we will take  some audience questions and the bonus  show this week is a good one 